's or d's if  it's any higher than an e if it's an f  or a g  then you cannot rent the house out um  because it means that it's not insulated  profit properly  the energy performance is so bad that  they say you can't rent a property out  that's anything higher than an e  however what the government are  introducing is they're saying that  they're going to increase the standard  of epc  ratings from an e to a c  what do i think about that well for a  start it's going to affect a lot of  landlords because i think about a third  of landlords think that they're going to  be affected by this because a lot of  property especially older style  properties like victorian houses a lot  of them are d's  some of them are even ease  so the fact they're saying it has to be  a minimum of a c  is going to mean that  a lot of landlords are going to have to  spend some money and it's going to be  that it could be thousands of pounds to  bring down the rating to make it run  more effectively they might have to  maybe get solar panels or  i mean to be fair  listen you can moan about these things  and you can say isn't it terrible oh  some of my properties won't comply i'm  have to spend money  and i understand why landlords are upset  but there are some things that you just  can't control i'm not going to start a  petition and say oh it's terrible  because actually it's not a bad thing  that houses are more efficient so  i'm not going to stand here and start a  petition and say the government  shouldn't change the the standard uh  neither am i going to moan and say oh  i'm sure some of my properties  many of them are d's and i'll have to  try and  bring it make it better and maybe spend  money to a c but at the same time um  our rents are going up at the moment  interest rates are very very low and  there's some mortgage lenders like the  mortgage works who are saying that  they're gonna they're gonna um  reward people for bringing their energy  performance certificates better they're  actually offering  1.5  interest payments which is just  ridiculously low so  i think rather than moaning about i've  got to spend three grand five grand  whatever i would probably say there are  some landlords that are going to sell up  which again creates opportunity  um  there's opportunity and disappointment  it's a combination but you're always  better off seeing the opportunity it's  good to have properties that have got a  good energy performance certificate it's  good for you it's good for the tenants  it's good for the environment um so  overall am i worried  not too worried especially as we've been  given four years notice  they're also the government are offering  grants  so i've done a video about this before  they're offering to pay towards any  property including landlords that are  looking to make their properties greener  so  overall i'm reasonably positive about it  even though it will be a little bit of  an inconvenience for sure so that's my  thoughts i hope that helps andrew let me  know what you think let me know  generally what do you think do you think  it's a good thing a bad thing what do  you think of my opinion comment below  i'd love to hear your thoughts and  opinions  michael day says  need your advice i've got a new job in  bristol and i'm buying a house on a  residential mortgage  and let out the spare is letting out the  spare room a good idea as i already have  a house down in dorset okay so if you're  buying a house in bristol to live in  and you've already got a property indoor  set  is it a good idea sure  if you can buy a house and then you can  put a lodger in and that lodge i mean  for a start you're gonna get bedroom  relief tax so you're not gonna have to  pay any tax for the first seven and a  half thousand pounds a year which will  probably be the entire amount of rent  from the lodge so it's tax-free income  secondly if you've got permission to do  it  that money is probably going to pay for  your mortgage payments so i