 when creating a second room  then you wouldn't need planning  permission for that but if you're going  to actually create a window  you may need planning permission i think  it would depend on for a start does the  window is this window going to be  overlooking is it going to be affecting  people's privacy i definitely wouldn't  just go and do it straight away without  speaking because also if you're going to  be building a window in place um i would  speak to building control i would speak  to the council um it it depends whether  you need planning permission you may not  um i'd have to i'd have to see it maybe  i definitely would speak though to a  planning consultant before you just  start doing the work because if you  carry out work and you hope that you  don't need planning and then it turns  out that you did need planning but you  didn't get planning  they can  make you  undo all of the work which is just such  a demoralizing thing to have to do so  don't just do it but i'm sure you'll be  able to get permissions i'm sure it'd be  absolutely fine and i hope that it adds  a lot of value to your house  okay matt bex  how you doing matt i hope you are well  matt says what are the average interest  rates and fees with bridging loans if a  brr project is six months what are the  average costs  so bridging loans  really vary typically you're probably  going to be talking about 12  per annum so if it's just for six months  you're probably going to pay about six  percent on that there may be an in an  in-and-out fee an exit fee um so they  sometimes might charge you one percent  um a one percent exit fee so  you're probably talking about six eight  percent of the money that you're  borrowing if you're only for six months  but of course it really depends on you  it depends on the deal it depends on the  lender but anywhere between  10 and 15  would be about normal per annum for a  bridging loan  all right last question i hope you're  enjoying this by the way do hit t